  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday Night through Friday)\nIssued at 1056 PM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nAn upper-level trough will move out of northern Mexico and track \nacross the Red River and into the Lower Mississippi Valley late \nSaturday night through late Sunday night. Global models and \nensembles agree that Gulf moisture and lift will spread north into\nthe area ahead of the approaching upper-level system Saturday in \nto Saturday night. Consensus in model data show the best lift and \ndynamics over our area will occur as the center of the trough/low \nmoves over southwest Oklahoma early Sunday morning. The National \nBlend of Models (NBM) continues to carry the highest PoPs (70-90%)\nacross the southwestern half of the CWA from early Sunday morning\nthrough Sunday afternoon. Total rainfall amounts will likely vary\nnoticeably from southwest to northeast. The highest amounts (0.75\nto 1.25 inches) through Sunday night will likely occur in a zone \nfrom southern Creek County to Choctaw County, due to closer \nproximity to the mid-level deformation zone, stronger low-level \njet convergence, and higher PWATs (near 1.0 inch). Locations north\nand east of this zone are expected to receive lighter totals, \ngenerally a few hundredths of an inch (far northeast Oklahoma and \nfar northwest Arkansas) to around a third of an inch. Did not\nmention any thunderstorm with this system, though limited \ninstability may cause a few rumbles of thunder mainly confined\nnear the Red River in far southeast Oklahoma.\n\nRain will end from west to east overnight Sunday night into \nMonday morning as the trough exits. High pressure will build back \ninto the Plains early next week. Overnight lows will trend warmer \nbeginning Monday night, with lows dipping into the mid to upper \n30s. A handful of colder-prone areas in far northeast Oklahoma and\nfar northwest Arkansas may briefly dip to or just below freezing.\nBy mid to late next week, ensemble guidance favors upper-level \nridging building over the central CONUS. Southerly winds will \nbring a warming trend, pushing afternoon highs back into the mid \nto upper 60s Wednesday and back into the lower 70s by the end of \nthe week.\n\nMejia",
